{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0178vWdKYcydbs5Spz8hVH1r","type":"tool_result","content":"=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/01-movie-list.md ===\n# 01: Movie list and day picker\n\n**What to build:** A customer opens `/`, picks a day with the day picker (five day buttons and a datepicker), and sees the movies playing that day with their screening times, loaded from the backend, with loading, error and empty states. The selected day lives in the URL. Clicking a screening opens its seat map route. Also sets up the five routes from section 3 (screens 2–5 may be empty placeholders for now) and the redirect of unknown URLs to `/`.\n\n**Specification:** sections 1, 2, 3 (routes), 3.3, 4, 9.1.\n\n**Blocked by:** None (can start immediately).\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-01 — Without a chosen date, `/` shows today's screenings: one movie card per movie with a screening today.\n- [ ] AC-02 — Each card shows title, genre, runtime in minutes and format (`PL dubbing` / `PL subtitles`).\n- [ ] AC-03 — Each card renders one button per screening of that movie on the selected day, labelled with the screening time (`HH:mm`), ordered by time.\n- [ ] AC-04 — Clicking a screening button navigates to `/screenings/:id/seats` for that screening id.\n- [ ] AC-49 — While the movie request is in flight, `/` shows a loading indicator and no movie cards.\n- [ ] AC-50 — When the movie request fails, `/` shows `Failed to load movies` and a `Retry` button that re-issues the request.\n- [ ] AC-52 — Above the movie list, the day picker shows `Today` and the next four days by English weekday name, followed by a datepicker; `Today` is selected by default.\n- [ ] AC-53 — Choosing another day shows only the movies with a screening on that day, each with only that day's screenings.\n- [ ] AC-54 — A day without screenings shows `No screenings on this day` and no movie cards.\n- [ ] AC-55 — The selected day is kept in the URL as `?date=YYYY-MM-DD`; opening such a URL selects that day, and a missing, invalid or past date selects today.\n- [ ] AC-51 — Every movie-list identifier in section 9.1 is present with the exact names and values given there.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/02-auditorium-map.md ===\n# 02: Auditorium map\n\n**What to build:** `/screenings/:id/seats` loads the screening and its movie and draws the page header (title, show date, seat-limit text), the `SCREEN` bar and the auditorium: 15 rows with row labels on both edges, both aisles in every row, the entrance gap in rows 1–10, accessible seats in row 1 and 12 couches in row 15. Occupied seats are shown as unavailable. Selection comes in ticket 03. An unknown screening id redirects to `/`.\n\n**Specification:** sections 3.3, 5 (intro and layout), 5.1, 5.3 (colours of available, unavailable and accessible seats), 9.2, 9.7.\n\n**Blocked by:** 01 — Movie list and day picker.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-05 — The seat map has 15 rows, numbered 1 to 15, row 1 rendered directly under the screen.\n- [ ] AC-06 — Rows 11 to 15 contain 24 columns; rows 1 to 10 contain columns 5 to 24 only.\n- [ ] AC-07 — A two-seat-wide gap is rendered between columns 4 and 5 and between columns 20 and 21 in every row; gaps carry no seat and no number.\n- [ ] AC-08 — Column numbering is global 1–24 and identical across rows; in rows 1–10, numbering starts at 5.\n- [ ] AC-09 — The row number is rendered on both the left and the right edge of every row.\n- [ ] AC-10 — Every seat renders its column number.\n- [ ] AC-11 — A `SCREEN` indicator is rendered above row 1.\n- [ ] AC-12 — Row 1, columns 10 to 15 are accessible seats — 6 of them.\n- [ ] AC-13 — Row 15 renders 12 couches, each spanning two adjacent columns: 1–2, 3–4, … 23–24.\n- [ ] AC-14 — A couch is rendered as one wide element, visually distinct from a single seat.\n- [ ] AC-15 — The total number of selectable elements in the auditorium is 308 (200 seats in rows 1–10, 96 in rows 11–14, 12 couches in row 15).\n- [ ] AC-56 — The seat selection shows the title `Choose your seats` and the subtitle `Show Date: <Weekday> <D> <Mon> <HH:mm>` of the screening.\n- [ ] AC-57 — Above the seat map, horizontally centred over it, the text `You can reserve up to 10 seats per reservation` is shown.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/03-seat-selection.md ===\n# 03: Seat selection, legend and selected-seats panel\n\n**What to build:** On the seat map the customer selects and deselects seats and couches, up to 10 seats per booking with a couch counting as 2. Unavailable seats cannot be selected. Exceeding the limit shows a toast. Every state has its colour, the legend explains them, and the selected-seats panel lists the current selection by row.\n\n**Specification:** sections 3.2, 5.2, 5.3, 5.5, 9.2, 9.6, 9.7.\n\n**Blocked by:** 02 — Auditorium map.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-16 — Seats listed in `occupiedSeats` for the screening are rendered unavailable and cannot be selected.\n- [ ] AC-17 — A couch is unavailable when either of its two seat identifiers is in `occupiedSeats`.\n- [ ] AC-18 — Clicking an available seat selects it; clicking it again deselects it.\n- [ ] AC-19 — Clicking a couch selects both of its seats as one unit; it cannot be half-selected.\n- [ ] AC-20 — Selection is capped at 10 seats; a couch counts as 2.\n- [ ] AC-21 — A click that would exceed 10 seats is ignored and shows the toast `Maximum 10 seats per booking`.\n- [ ] AC-22 — At the cap, already selected seats and couches can still be deselected.\n- [ ] AC-23 — The legend lists exactly `Available`, `Selected`, `Unavailable`, `Accessible`.\n- [ ] AC-24 — Available seats use `#2E7D32`.\n- [ ] AC-25 — Selected seats use `#F9A825`.\n- [ ] AC-26 — Unavailable seats use `#C62828`.\n- [ ] AC-27 — Accessible seats use `#1565C0`.\n- [ ] AC-58 — Between the seat map and the `Cancel` / `Continue` buttons, the selected-seats panel shows `Selected seats: <n>` and one `Row <r>: <seats>` line per row, updated on every selection change.\n- [ ] AC-66 — Every seat-selection identifier in section 9.2, and the toast identifier in section 9.6, is present with the exact names and values given there.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/04-booking-summary.md ===\n# 04: Booking and summary\n\n**What to build:** The selection becomes a booking kept in `sessionStorage`. `Continue` takes the customer to the summary (`Your tickets`), which lists each ticket with its price, a `Remove` button and the total; `Back` returns to the seat map with the selection intact and `Cancel` on the seat map clears the booking. The summary and checkout routes redirect to `/` without a matching booking.\n\n**Specification:** sections 3.1, 5.4, 5.6, 6, 9.3, 9.7.\n\n**Blocked by:** 03 — Seat selection, legend and selected-seats panel.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-28 — Seats in rows 1–5 cost `25.00 PLN`.\n- [ ] AC-29 — Seats in rows 6–10 cost `32.00 PLN`.\n- [ ] AC-30 — Seats in rows 11–14 cost `39.00 PLN`.\n- [ ] AC-31 — A couch costs `89.00 PLN` per couch, not per seat.\n- [ ] AC-32 — Accessible seats cost `25.00 PLN`, like the rest of row 1.\n- [ ] AC-33 — `Cancel` navigates to `/` and clears the selection.\n- [ ] AC-34 — `Continue` navigates to `/screenings/:id/summary`.\n- [ ] AC-35 — `Continue` does nothing when no seat is selected.\n- [ ] AC-36 — Each selected item is listed with movie title, screening date, time, row number, seat description and price.\n- [ ] AC-37 — A couch is described as `Couch <a>-<b>`; a single seat is described by its column number.\n- [ ] AC-38 — The total line reads `Total: <sum> PLN` and equals the sum of the listed prices.\n- [ ] AC-39 — `Back` returns to the seat map with the selection intact.\n- [ ] AC-40 — `Confirm` navigates to `/screenings/:id/checkout`.\n- [ ] AC-59 — The summary shows the title `Your tickets`.\n- [ ] AC-60 — Each summary item has a `Remove` button that removes it from the booking and updates the total; removing the last item returns to the seat map.\n- [ ] AC-67 — Every summary identifier in section 9.3 is present with the exact names and values given there.\n- [ ] The booking survives a reload of the seat map and of the summary.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/05-checkout.md ===\n# 05: Checkout and payment\n\n**What to build:** The customer fills in their details on the checkout form (`Your details`), sees validation messages as they go, can go `Back` to the summary and return — or reload — without losing what they typed, and pays with the `Pay <total> PLN` button. Paying sends the reservation to the backend; success opens `/confirmation`, failure keeps the form and shows a toast.\n\n**Specification:** sections 2.1, 2.2 (`POST /reservations`), 3.1, 7, 9.4, 9.7.\n\n**Blocked by:** 04 — Booking and summary.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-41 — The form renders first name, last name, email, confirm email, phone number and a terms checkbox.\n- [ ] AC-42 — First name and last name are required and must be at least 2 characters.\n- [ ] AC-43 — Email is required and must be a valid address; confirm email must match it.\n- [ ] AC-44 — Phone number is optional; when filled it must be exactly 9 digits.\n- [ ] AC-45 — The terms checkbox is required.\n- [ ] AC-46 — The pay button is enabled whenever no payment request is in flight; clicking it on an invalid form marks all fields as touched, shows the messages and shows the toast `The form contains errors`, without navigating.\n- [ ] AC-47 — The pay button on a valid form sends `POST /reservations`, is disabled while the request is in flight, navigates to `/confirmation` on success, and on failure stays on the form and shows the toast `Payment failed. Please try again.`\n- [ ] AC-61 — The checkout shows the title `Your details`.\n- [ ] AC-62 — The pay button reads `Pay <total> PLN`, the total of the booking.\n- [ ] AC-63 — `Back` on the checkout returns to the summary with the booking intact.\n- [ ] AC-64 — The checkout values survive going back to the summary and forward again, and a page reload; a new booking starts with an empty form.\n- [ ] AC-65 — The labels of the five required fields end with `*`; the phone label does not.\n- [ ] AC-68 — Every checkout identifier in section 9.4 is present with the exact names and values given there.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/06-confirmation.md ===\n# 06: Confirmation and booking lifecycle\n\n**What to build:** After a successful payment the customer sees the confirmation with their email address; `Continue` clears the booking and returns to `/`. The whole booking lifecycle from section 3.1 holds: it survives reloads, and every route guards against a missing or mismatched booking.\n\n**Specification:** sections 3.1, 8, 9.5.\n\n**Blocked by:** 05 — Checkout and payment.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-48 — `/confirmation` shows `Payment successful` and `Your tickets will be sent shortly to <email>`; `Continue` navigates to `/` and clears the booking; the booking survives a reload via `sessionStorage`; entering the summary, checkout or confirmation without a matching booking redirects to `/`.\n- [ ] AC-69 — Every confirmation identifier in section 9.5 is present with the exact names and values given there.\n- [ ] Reloading `/confirmation` after paying still shows the confirmation.\n- [ ] After `Continue`, the seat map of the same screening shows no selected seats and the checkout form of a new booking is empty.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/07-final-pass.md ===\n# 07: Final pass\n\n**What to build:** A walk through the whole flow — movie list, seat map, summary, checkout,\nconfirmation — against the specification, fixing whatever does not match.\n\n**Specification:** the whole of `README.md`, section 9 in particular.\n\n**Blocked by:** 01–06.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] Every acceptance criterion in section 10 is met.\n- [ ] `pnpm nx build cinescope` succeeds.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5476e868-cc4f-4da9-a31d-ea08c408bea6","uuid":"0e2b9f1a-8f2e-4011-9972-5bf0e20238bb","timestamp":"2026-10-20T08:29:33.177Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/01-movie-list.md ===\n# 01: Movie list and day picker\n\n**What to build:** A customer opens `/`, picks a day with the day picker (five day buttons and a datepicker), and sees the movies playing that day with their screening times, loaded from the backend, with loading, error and empty states. The selected day lives in the URL. Clicking a screening opens its seat map route. Also sets up the five routes from section 3 (screens 2–5 may be empty placeholders for now) and the redirect of unknown URLs to `/`.\n\n**Specification:** sections 1, 2, 3 (routes), 3.3, 4, 9.1.\n\n**Blocked by:** None (can start immediately).\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-01 — Without a chosen date, `/` shows today's screenings: one movie card per movie with a screening today.\n- [ ] AC-02 — Each card shows title, genre, runtime in minutes and format (`PL dubbing` / `PL subtitles`).\n- [ ] AC-03 — Each card renders one button per screening of that movie on the selected day, labelled with the screening time (`HH:mm`), ordered by time.\n- [ ] AC-04 — Clicking a screening button navigates to `/screenings/:id/seats` for that screening id.\n- [ ] AC-49 — While the movie request is in flight, `/` shows a loading indicator and no movie cards.\n- [ ] AC-50 — When the movie request fails, `/` shows `Failed to load movies` and a `Retry` button that re-issues the request.\n- [ ] AC-52 — Above the movie list, the day picker shows `Today` and the next four days by English weekday name, followed by a datepicker; `Today` is selected by default.\n- [ ] AC-53 — Choosing another day shows only the movies with a screening on that day, each with only that day's screenings.\n- [ ] AC-54 — A day without screenings shows `No screenings on this day` and no movie cards.\n- [ ] AC-55 — The selected day is kept in the URL as `?date=YYYY-MM-DD`; opening such a URL selects that day, and a missing, invalid or past date selects today.\n- [ ] AC-51 — Every movie-list identifier in section 9.1 is present with the exact names and values given there.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/02-auditorium-map.md ===\n# 02: Auditorium map\n\n**What to build:** `/screenings/:id/seats` loads the screening and its movie and draws the page header (title, show date, seat-limit text), the `SCREEN` bar and the auditorium: 15 rows with row labels on both edges, both aisles in every row, the entrance gap in rows 1–10, accessible seats in row 1 and 12 couches in row 15. Occupied seats are shown as unavailable. Selection comes in ticket 03. An unknown screening id redirects to `/`.\n\n**Specification:** sections 3.3, 5 (intro and layout), 5.1, 5.3 (colours of available, unavailable and accessible seats), 9.2, 9.7.\n\n**Blocked by:** 01 — Movie list and day picker.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-05 — The seat map has 15 rows, numbered 1 to 15, row 1 rendered directly under the screen.\n- [ ] AC-06 — Rows 11 to 15 contain 24 columns; rows 1 to 10 contain columns 5 to 24 only.\n- [ ] AC-07 — A two-seat-wide gap is rendered between columns 4 and 5 and between columns 20 and 21 in every row; gaps carry no seat and no number.\n- [ ] AC-08 — Column numbering is global 1–24 and identical across rows; in rows 1–10, numbering starts at 5.\n- [ ] AC-09 — The row number is rendered on both the left and the right edge of every row.\n- [ ] AC-10 — Every seat renders its column number.\n- [ ] AC-11 — A `SCREEN` indicator is rendered above row 1.\n- [ ] AC-12 — Row 1, columns 10 to 15 are accessible seats — 6 of them.\n- [ ] AC-13 — Row 15 renders 12 couches, each spanning two adjacent columns: 1–2, 3–4, … 23–24.\n- [ ] AC-14 — A couch is rendered as one wide element, visually distinct from a single seat.\n- [ ] AC-15 — The total number of selectable elements in the auditorium is 308 (200 seats in rows 1–10, 96 in rows 11–14, 12 couches in row 15).\n- [ ] AC-56 — The seat selection shows the title `Choose your seats` and the subtitle `Show Date: <Weekday> <D> <Mon> <HH:mm>` of the screening.\n- [ ] AC-57 — Above the seat map, horizontally centred over it, the text `You can reserve up to 10 seats per reservation` is shown.\n\n=== /workspace/project/spec/tickets/03-seat-selection.md ===\n# 03: Seat selection, legend and selected-seats panel\n\n**What to build:** On the seat map the customer selects and deselects seats and couches, up to 10 seats per booking with a couch counting as 2. Unavailable seats cannot be selected. Exceeding the limit shows a toast. Every state has its colour, the legend explains them, and the selected-seats panel lists the current selection by row.\n\n**Specification:** sections 3.2, 5.2, 5.3, 5.5, 9.2, 9.6, 9.7.\n\n**Blocked by:** 02 — Auditorium map.\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n- [ ] AC-16 — Seats listed in `occupiedSeats` for the screening are rendered unavailable and cannot be selected.\n- [ ] AC-17 — A couch is unavailable when either of its two seat identifiers is in `occupiedSeats`.\n- [ ] AC-18 — Clicking an available seat selects it; clicking it again deselects it.\n- [ ] AC-19 — Clicking a couch selects both of its seats as one unit; it cannot be half-selected.\n- [ ] AC-20 — Selection is capped at 10 seats; a couch counts as 2.\n- [ ] AC-21 — A click that would exceed 10 seats is ignored and shows the toast `Maximum 10 seats per booking`.\n- [ ] AC-22 — At the cap, already selected seats and couches can still be deselected.\n- [ ] AC-23 — The legend lists exactly `Available`, `Selected`, `Unavailable`, `Accessible`.\n- [ ] AC-24 — Available seats use `#2E7D32`.\n- [ ] AC-25 — Selected seats use `#F9A825`.\n- [ ] AC-26 — Unavailable seats use `#C62828`.\n- [ ] AC-27 — Accessible seats use `#1565C0`.\n- [ ] AC-58 — Between the seat map and the `Cancel` / `Continue` buttons, the selected-seats panel shows `Selected seats: <n>` and one `Row <r>: <seats>` line per row, updated on every selection change.\n- [ ] AC-